   1: spec:
   2:   task_type: move_along_path (triangle sprites emit projectiles that land 5 cells away)
   3:   grid:
   4:     shape: 16 rows x 16 cols (fixed)
   5:     bg: 7 (fixed)
   6:   sprite:
   7:     count: 4-9 triangular sprites per grid
   8:     color: single non-bg color shared by all sprites; chosen from {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9} (any color != 7)
   9:     size: exactly 3 pixels each (8-connected component)
  10:     shapes: |
  11:       Two canonical 3-pixel shapes (solver classifies by 4-neighbor corner degree):
  12:         L-shape (axis-aligned right-angle, fills 3 corners of a 2x2 box):
  13:           corner pixel has 2 4-neighbors; fires DIAGONALLY away from the missing 2x2 corner.
  14:           Example: {(0,0),(0,1),(1,0)} -> missing (1,1); apex=(0,0); dir=(-1,-1) up-left
  15:         V-shape (apex + two diagonal legs, spans 2 rows x 3 cols or 3 rows x 2 cols):
  16:           apex has 2 8-neighbors (both diagonal); fires AXIS-ALIGNED opposite the opening.
  17:           Example: {(0,1),(1,0),(1,2)} -> apex=(0,1); dir=(-1,0) up
  18:     direction: 8 possible (4 diagonal for L, 4 axis for V); any mix of kinds and directions per grid
  19:     separation: min Chebyshev distance between any two sprites >= 2 (no 8-connected merging)
  20:   projectile:
  21:     target_cell: apex + 5 * dir
  22:     in_bounds: target must lie within the 16x16 grid for every sprite
  23:   transform:
  24:     grid_shape: same
  25:     hit_sprite: if target cell lies on ANY sprite, recolor ENTIRE hit sprite to 9 (maroon)
  26:     multi_hit_bg: if >=2 projectiles target the same EMPTY bg cell, paint that cell 1 (blue)
  27:     lone_hit_bg: else a single projectile on an empty bg cell paints it 9 (maroon)
  28:     preserve: unhit sprites keep their original color; untouched bg stays 7
  29:   event_richness [observed]:
  30:     at least one of {sprite-hit, multi-projectile collision} occurs per grid
  31:     typical mix: 0-3 sprite hits, 0-2 collision points, rest are lone bg markers
  32: 
  33: generation_strategy:
  34:   approach: output-first (place sprites + pre-chosen landing events, then erase landings for input)
  35:   steps:
  36:     - fix H=W=16, bg=7; pick sprite_color uniformly from {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9}
  37:     - decide sprite count N in [4,9] and a few events: e_hit in [0,2] (projectile hits another sprite), e_coll in [0,2] (2+ projectiles share a bg landing cell); remaining (N - #emitters_consumed_by_events) sprites land on unique empty bg cells
  38:     - pre-sample sprite positions AND their (kind, direction) jointly so that every apex+5*dir lies in-bounds; reject placements that would make two sprites 8-adjacent (Chebyshev < 2)
  39:     - for each "hit" event: pick a target sprite first, then add a shooter whose apex+5*dir lands on one of the target's 3 pixels (try all 8 directions; choose a kind compatible with that direction)
  40:     - for each "collision" event: pick a bg cell, then add 2+ shooters whose apex+5*dir equals that cell
  41:     - for remaining shooters: pick any apex+dir with target cell empty and not coinciding with other shooters' targets
  42:     - paint sprites into the grid using the canonical L/V pixel patterns for each (kind,dir); do NOT paint any landing markers -> this is the input
  43:     - run the solver to produce the expected output; assert: non-bg pixel count increases, at least one 9 and optionally 1 appears, and shape is preserved

